          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sōlstitium, sōlstitiī — \"the summer solstice; midsummer heat\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Sōlstitiō diēs longissimus est. At the summer solstice the day is longest."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Second declension, neuter: sōlstitium, sōlstitiī n. For Romans mostly the summer one; midwinter was brūma. English solstice comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sōlstitium is built from sōl 'sun' and sistō 'to stand still' (EtymDB; Lewis & Short): the time when the sun seems to stand still. English solstice comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
