          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "canīcula, canīculae — \"the Dog Star; a little dog\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ubi canīcula ardet, agricolae in umbrā quiēscunt. When the Dog Star blazes, the farmers rest in the shade."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "First declension, feminine: canīcula, canīculae f., a diminutive of canis 'dog'. Its rising in high summer brought the hottest days, the dog days."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "canīcula is a diminutive of canis 'dog' with -cula (EtymDB; Lewis & Short): a little dog, and the star in the mouth of the Great Dog. English canicular comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
