          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "caleō, calēre — \"to be warm, to be hot\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Aestāte saxa sub sōle calent. In summer the stones are hot under the sun."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "caleō, calēre, caluī, second conjugation. Calidus 'hot' and calor 'heat' come from it; English calorie comes from calor."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "caleō is inherited from Proto-Italic kalēō 'to be hot', from PIE ḱelh₁- 'to heat' (EtymDB). calidus 'hot' and calor 'heat', the source of English calorie, are built on it (Lewis & Short)."
            }
          ]
        },
